# Copy to dev.env (gitignored). All optional: with nothing set, the dev stack
# listens on localhost only, and seeds from teslamate-database-1.

#APP_TZ=Australia/Brisbane          # also the dashboard's default timezone
#SEED_FROM=teslamate-database-1     # your TeslaMate database container
#DEV_UI_PORT=8089
#DEV_GRAFANA_PORT=8090

# Where the scheduled jobs run in this stack: app (the default), worker (a second container, dev-worker, from
# the same image, as in production) or off.
#SCHEDULER=app

# Publish through Traefik instead of localhost. The app has no login of its own,
# so AUTH_MIDDLEWARE (a forward-auth middleware) is required with PUBLIC_HOST.
#PUBLIC_HOST=teslamate.example.com
#AUTH_MIDDLEWARE=authentik-forwardauth@docker
#PROXY_NETWORK=proxy
#PROXY_ENTRYPOINT=websecure

# Keep CLAUDE.local.md (local notes for Claude Code) as a copy of a file kept
# elsewhere, e.g. in a private repo. Refreshed by every dev.sh command.
#CLAUDE_LOCAL_SOURCE=/path/to/private/notes.md

# Where scripts/dev.sh onedrive-save keeps the saved dev OneDrive sign-in. Must be outside
# the repository. Default: ~/.config/garagebooks/onedrive-dev
#ONEDRIVE_DEV_FILE=/path/outside/the/repo/onedrive-dev

# The dev stack's invoice folder. Default: .dev/invoices in the checkout. To try the app on a real
# network share, mount it on this machine (a mount that is there before Docker needs it, e.g. an
# /etc/fstab line with x-systemd.automount and nofail), writable by you (the app runs as you), and set:
#DEV_INVOICES_SHARE=/mnt/share/invoices-dev
# Each stack (the main checkout, every worktree) then gets its own folder in it, named after the stack,
# made when the stack starts, so branches never mix their test PDFs. The share itself must be a real
# mount: an empty mount point is refused, never filled. `dev.sh destroy` leaves the folder on the share.
# DEV_INVOICES_DIR instead names exactly one folder, for every stack; it wins if both are set.
#DEV_INVOICES_DIR=/mnt/share/invoices-dev/one-folder

# The dev stack runs a stand-in claims connector (tests/fake_connector.py, invented answers only) and
# points the app at it, so the Claim pages work. To point a dev stack at your own connector instead, set
# these; the stand-in is then not used by the app.
# Sign with a key pair of its own that you can revoke, not your live one (the connector lists its public
# half), and preview only: a submit sends a real claim. The key stays in its file; only the path is saved here:
#DEV_CLAIMS_CONNECTOR_URL=http://host:8765
#DEV_CLAIMS_CONNECTOR_KEY_FILE=/path/to/dev-claims-key.pem
# With these set, `up` uses that connector and the browser tests refuse to run (they submit claims). To use
# the stand-in for a while: STAND_IN=1 scripts/dev.sh up, and STAND_IN=1 scripts/dev.sh test-browser.
